## Static
Sometimes data belongs to the class itself, not to any instance. Use `static`:

```ts
class Video {
    static platformName: string = "MeTube";
    static videoCount: number = 0;

    title: string;

    constructor(title: string) {
        this.title = title;
        Video.videoCount++;
    }
}

// no instance needed
console.log(Video.platformName);  // MeTube

new Video("Cat Falls");
new Video("Dog Runs");
console.log(Video.videoCount);  // 2
```

- `static` fields are accessed on the CLASS, not on instances
- `Video.videoCount` works, `myVideo.videoCount` does NOT
- Static fields are shared across all instances

## Getters & Setters
Getters and setters let you control how private fields are accessed:

```ts
class Channel {
    private _name: string;
    private _subscribers: number = 0;

    constructor(name: string) {
        this._name = name;
    }

    get name(): string {
        return this._name;
    }

    set name(value: string) {
        if (value.length === 0) throw new Error("Name can't be empty");
        this._name = value;
    }

    get subscribers(): number {
        return this._subscribers;
    }
}

const ch = new Channel("Steve");
console.log(ch.name);      // calls getter -> "Steve"
ch.name = "NewSteve";       // calls setter
ch.name = "";               // throws Error!
console.log(ch.subscribers); // 0, no setter = read only
```

- `get` makes a property readable: `ch.name` calls the getter
- `set` makes it writable with validation: `ch.name = x` calls the setter
- No setter = read-only property
- Convention: prefix private fields with `_`

